1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,814
Every day, businesses generate receipts.

2
00:00:03,814 --> 00:00:09,253
And behind every receipt, someone has to turn a piece of paper into an accounting transaction.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,458
Businesses already have their own financial systems.

4
00:00:13,458 --> 00:00:15,222
We didn't want to replace them.

5
00:00:15,222 --> 00:00:18,237
We wanted to automate the work happening inside them.

6
00:00:18,237 --> 00:00:19,546
Introducing LedgerFlow.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:23,772
Here is the real app.

8
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:28,436
You sign in, name your agent, and LedgerFlow sets up a workspace on your Mac.

9
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:35,135
Then you bring your own Excel workbook.

10
00:00:35,135 --> 00:00:40,130
LedgerFlow maps its accounts and the exact places it may write, and you confirm.

11
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:42,831
The iPhone is optional.

12
00:00:42,831 --> 00:00:43,679
Pair it once:

13
00:00:43,679 --> 00:00:48,441
the Mac shows a code, the phone scans it, and they talk over local Wi-Fi.

14
00:00:48,441 --> 00:00:50,854
Then scan a receipt wherever you are.

15
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:54,051
The phone reads the text and sends it to the Mac.

16
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:02,225
Seconds later, the receipt lands on the Mac.

17
00:01:02,225 --> 00:01:07,178
Vultr inference reads every field, and each value shows the text it came from.

18
00:01:07,178 --> 00:01:11,687
Here the receipt is incomplete, so LedgerFlow asks instead of guessing.

19
00:01:11,687 --> 00:01:14,291
Nothing is written until a human answers.

20
00:01:15,491 --> 00:01:21,103
Vultr Serverless Inference reads the receipt and turns it into structured financial information.

21
00:01:21,103 --> 00:01:25,546
Every value is linked to the text it came from, and still has to be checked.

22
00:01:26,746 --> 00:01:30,598
Financial decisions cannot depend on model confidence alone.

23
00:01:30,598 --> 00:01:37,852
Deterministic accounting rules build a balanced entry, and define the exact cells the agent is allowed to change.

24
00:01:39,052 --> 00:01:41,571
LedgerFlow doesn't just recommend an entry.

25
00:01:41,571 --> 00:01:45,028
It uses Vultr to generate a program that performs the work.

26
00:01:46,228 --> 00:01:50,379
Every generated program runs in a disposable sandbox.

27
00:01:50,379 --> 00:01:56,644
No network, no secrets, one CPU, five hundred twelve megabytes, a hard deadline.

28
00:01:56,644 --> 00:01:59,151
Then the container is destroyed.

29
00:02:00,451 --> 00:02:03,193
But what happens when the code misbehaves?

30
00:02:03,193 --> 00:02:09,332
Here, a hostile program tries the network, tries to overwrite its input, and then never stops.

31
00:02:09,332 --> 00:02:14,948
The sandbox blocks each attempt, kills it at the deadline, and destroys the container.

32
00:02:20,351 --> 00:02:21,784
Running is not enough.

33
00:02:21,784 --> 00:02:31,294
An independent verifier opens the resulting workbook, checks every change, keeps formulas and features intact, and confirms the entry is balanced.

34
00:02:32,494 --> 00:02:34,978
The status flows back to the phone.

35
00:02:34,978 --> 00:02:39,521
History, this month's total, and the same agent, in your pocket.

36
00:02:43,794 --> 00:02:47,497
When a decision is needed, LedgerFlow asks a person.

37
00:02:47,497 --> 00:02:49,491
A notification on the phone:

38
00:02:49,491 --> 00:02:50,488
review needed.

39
00:02:50,488 --> 00:02:53,123
The agent prepares, a human approves.

40
00:02:54,794 --> 00:02:57,578
The accountant sees exactly what will change.

41
00:02:57,578 --> 00:03:02,588
Approval is bound to the verified file itself, not to the model's recommendation.

42
00:03:03,788 --> 00:03:05,994
The result is a real Excel workbook.

43
00:03:05,994 --> 00:03:10,774
Eight cells written, nothing else touched, and the original version preserved.

44
00:03:11,974 --> 00:03:21,828
We compared eighteen model configurations on real Vultr inference, attacked the sandbox with hostile programs, and measured how well existing workbooks are preserved.

45
00:03:25,474 --> 00:03:32,292
Cloud inference, deterministic controls, sandboxed execution, and independent verification.

46
00:03:32,292 --> 00:03:37,387
An agent that doesn't just answer questions, but performs real work.

47
00:03:38,587 --> 00:03:40,918
Your employees capture the receipts.

48
00:03:40,918 --> 00:03:42,795
LedgerFlow performs the work.

49
00:03:42,795 --> 00:03:43,507
LedgerFlow.

50
00:03:43,507 --> 00:03:46,032
Every receipt lands in the right cells.

51
00:03:46,032 --> 00:03:46,744
With proof.

52
00:03:50,587 --> 00:03:51,901
LedgerFlow is in beta.

53
00:03:51,901 --> 00:03:54,827
Try it with your own receipts, on your own Excel.

